PLN 105 million for companies in the video games industry from the National Centre for Research and Development
Almost PLN 105 million of co-finance will go to companies from the gaming industry, creating innovative solutions.
The National Centre for Research and Development announced on Friday the results of the 3rd edition of the GameINN program.
Nearly 30 projects supported by the NCBR include the development of a platform for the creation of field games based on an AR game engine creating a map of the real-world together with an application enabling the transmission of data by the users; a testing and training platform to verify practical skills in the workplace and professional development with the use of VR and AI technology; an innovative game simulating the management of real production processes using an innovative model of gameplay based on the interaction of the virtual industry.
GameINN’s sectoral programme aims to increase the competitiveness of the Polish sector of video game producers on the global market until 2023.
According to the NCBR, the Polish video games sector is already growing at a rate of 10 per cent a year.
According to government data, the value of the Polish video game market in 2016 amounted to PLN 1.85 billion, and the forecast for 2019 is 2.23 billion.
“Specially prepared GameINN’s research agenda and already reaching PLN 300 million in support for entrepreneurs as part of this sectoral program are an important impetuous impetus for the further, dynamic growth of the Polish gamedevu,” the release reads.
The video games industry is an excellent example of the effective use of the potential that we have at our disposal in Poland.
“Includes introduced by the government to innovative entrepreneurs, such as R&D reliefs, competency improvement programs, IP box or support programs implemented by the NCBR, drive the innovative activities of companies and allow them to compete more effectively on a very competitive market,” says Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Science and Higher Education Jarosław Gowin.
The head of the Ministry of Science points out that money for innovation not only goes to innovators in the form of co-subsidividing projects, but also stays in their pockets – three-quarters more entrepreneurs benefited from R&D relief in 2018 than a year earlier.
“The amount of deducted costs related to R&D activities amounted to PLN 1.7 billion, which resulted in over PLN 300 million in savings for companies benefiting from this relief.
“It’s the equivalent of support provided by the NCBR in three editions of GameINN,” the Deputy Prime Minister emphasizes.
The director of the NCBR, Wojciech Kamieniecki, recalls that in the two previous competitions co-financials with a total value of over PLN 111.2 million were awarded to 78 innovative projects.
Huge interest in the 3rd competition as well as the variety of submitted applications show that the video game industry has ideas and competence to further develop and increase its competitive advantages.
“Especially pleases the fact that among the selected projects – as in the previous editions – there are projects concerning solutions for not only entertainment, but also related to industry 4.0,” he adds.
The 3rd GameINN for co-finance of up to PLN 20 million could be claimed by entrepreneurs, consortia of entrepreneurs and consortia of entrepreneurs with scientific units.
The NCBR received 87 applications for a total co-finance amount of about PLN 317 million.
The budget of the competition at the time of the announcement of the competition amounted to PLN 100 million, but it was increased, thanks to which 27 projects were granted nearly PLN 105 million in co-finance.
Detailed information about the results of the 3rd GameINN competition can be found at ncbr.gov.pl.
GameINN’s sector program is implemented as part of the Smart Growth Operational Programme.
NCBR launched the program in 2016 in response to the initiative of entrepreneurs associated in the Polskie Góra Agreement, which includes companies such as CD Projekt, Techland, CI Games, Bloober Team or 11 bit studios.
Lamine Diaby-Fadiga pleads guilty to steal Kasper Dolberg watch
