Barbie against stereotypes.
Gender dolls
The manufacturer Barbie has launched a gender-neut-ne-continuous puppet series.
In this way, he wants to combat stereotypes.
Manufacturer Barbie Launches Gender Neutral Dolpoter
In addition to Barbie with a different skin color, figure or hairstyle, Mattel has launched gender-neutral dolls.
“Creatable World” toys wear wig wigs.
Therefore, children can decide whether their Barbie should have short hair or long.
Their wardrobe consists of both trouser trousers and dresses.
In the blink of an eye, dolls can become boys and vice versa.
They are also available in a variety of skin colors and with different clothes.
Emphasizing the distinctive sexual characteristics has also been resigned—gender dolls no longer have full lips, long eyelashes and slim waist or, in the case of Ken, broad shoulders.
“The company believes that children don’t like when their dolls have gender pretenses, and some parents and feminists are convinced that toys made with a specific sex in mind hampers girls’ ambitions and amplify traditional stereotypes.
“The new dolls will allow all children to feel free,” Mattel said.
The Barbie doll, which appeared on the market in 1959 in a swimsuit and high-heeled shoes, has in the past been repeatedly criticized for propagating “unhealthy female stereotypes”, such as an unnatural body ratio.
“The company has reacted to critics and has already launched dolls of different skin colors, as well as more rounded shapes.
Dolls also appeared in the hijab.
