Dakar: West Africa's largest mosque opens
Approximately tens of thousands of people from all over Senegal gathered on Friday in the inauguration of a huge mosque, considered the largest in West Africa.
The Muslims flocked by buses, cars, and walk to the Bopp district, where a new mosque could accommodate 30,000 worshippers.
The building was built by the Mouride Brotherhood, belonging to the Sufi movement of Islam that dominates in Senegal.
Work on the Mosque began ten years ago in a wet six hectare area handed over by the government.
Massalikul Jinan’s theme derives from Sheikh Ahmadou Bamba Mbacke, a nineteenth-century fraternityfounder veneration venerated by believers as a saint.
The Mosque is tied with Karrian marble and has five minarets.
The tallest of them is 78 meters.
The Mosque can accommodate 15,000 worshippers in the middle, and the latter can stay on the outer esplanad.
In richly decorated interiors you can admire the gilded dome, giant chandeliers and hand-made decorations made by Moroccan workers.
The builders say the mosque is the largest in West Africa, but the edifice does not have a comparison to mosques in other regions.
The Hassana II Mosque can accommodate 105,000 worshippers in Morocco in Casablanca.
The construction of the mosque, which costs more than EUR 30 million, was financed from private donations.
The government, in addition to the ground, on the other hand, provided lighting, sanimodators and financed 10.5-million-euro road works.
