These Trees Can Disappear Completely from Europe
“42 per cent of the tree-setting tree species in Europe are endangered,” the International Conservation Conservation Union (IUCN) warns the International Conservation Union (IUCN) in the “Red Book of European Trees.”
At the forefront of the list are, among others, chestnut trees and cherry.
IUCN, an organisation headquartered in Gland, Switzerland, publishes a list of endangered European tree species for the first time.
It has been complied with specialists who, on the basis of the research, came to the conclusion that out of the 454 tree species occurring in Europe (as well as in other parts of the world), 42% are “highly endangered”.
Among Cause The Red Paper cites the rapid growth of urban agglomerations and global climate change.
However, the most endangered are those trees and shrubs, which occur only or mainly on our continent.
In terms of a typical European stand, according to IUCN specialists, 58% of species are endangered and 15 per cent, i.e. 66 species of trees and shrubs, are at risk of extinction.
Among the main causes of deforestation, the Red Paper lists errors in the exploitation of the stands, accelerated urbanization, too intensive felling of trees, followed by forest fires and changes taking place in the European eco-stigism.
According to the authors of the report, the existence of a number of European varieties of shrubs, mosses and ligaments is also severely endangered.
“Consequences of human activities lead to the gradual depletion of European plant species and pose in Europe the danger of extinction of important and underappreciated varieties, which form the backbone of European ecosystems and have an impact on the health of our on-the-mos on the planet,” concluded a report by Luc Bas, which heads the European IUCN bureau.
