GUS: In Poland we are living more and better
According to a report on household budgets published by the Central Statistical Office (GUS) on Friday, 44% of them assessed their financial situation as a good or rather good material situation last year.
A year earlier this figure was 37.2 per cent, in 2016, 33.55 per cent, and earlier – since 2009, when the Statistical Office first investigated subjective assessments of the situation of households – it never exceeded 30 per cent.
A total of 8.1 per cent of households, compared to 11.2 per cent a year earlier and more than 20 per cent in 2009, considered a bad or rather implicious material situation last year.
The financial situation of self-emborted households is best.
More than 70 per cent of the respondents consider it good in this group.
At the other pole are recicated households, where the percentage of such responses amounted to only 18%.
The subjective assessment of the financial situation of households is also strongly influenced by the number of children.
It is the poorest for single parents, of whom 32% declare a good or rather good material situation.
Among couples with children this percentage ranges (depending on the number of children) from 55 to 60 per cent, and among childless households is 48.8 per cent.
In these statistics there is clearly visible the effect of the 500+ program.
Before it was triggered (in 2016) the worth of their financial situation was judged by single-minded parents and large-child families (with at least three children).
Improving the subjective assessments of the financial situation of Poles is a reflection of the rapid increase in income, primarily from work.
However, that’s not all, because the percentage of households, who consider their situation to be good, increases faster than real incomes.
What’s more, there is no clear relationship at the level of voivodeships between the level of income and the assessment of the material conditions of households.
The best assessments are formulated by the inhabitants of the Opole voivoivoivoivoivoivoivo voivodship, where 48.9 percent of the respondents consider their situation as good or rather good.
Next are Lubuskie voivodships (47.7 per cent), śląskie (47.6 per cent), Mazovia voivodship (47.3 per cent).
Meanwhile, in Opolski, disposable income (i. i.e. after paying taxes and contributions) was less than 94% of the national average in Lubuskie.
Appropriators of Mazovia (120 per cent of the national average) and the Sląskie (104%) had the highest income.
On the average in Poland disposable income per person was PLN 1,693 in 2018.
In real terms (follow the impact of price changes), it increased by 4.3 per cent compared to 2017.
Compared to previous years, it is quite a low result.
In 2016, income per person increased by 7% and a year later by 6.3 percent.
Although an increase in income is declining, the increase in spending is also hampered.
Those per capita amounted to PLN 1,187 last year, which means that in real terms they decreased by 0.7%.
It’s the first such case since 2013.
In 2017, spending per person actually increased by 1.9 per cent, and in 2016 by 4.3 per cent.
Household budget surveys suggest that the household currently spends 70.1 percent of its disposable income, while in 2014 its share exceeded 80 per cent and exceeded 90 per cent by 2006.
