Vote on the break in the deliberations during Conservative Conference
British parliamentarians voted against a three-day hiatus from the House of Commons for the annual Conservative Party's annual programming conference, as requested by the government.
There were 289 MEPs against – 306.
Normally, the House of Municipalities does not meet during party conferences, so that MEPs do not have to give up either their participation or the meetings of the Parliament.
However, the opposition parties have acknowledged that in such a crucial time the last few weeks prior to the UK’s exit from the European Union scheduled for October 31, the parliament should not halt the deliberations.
289 deputies voted in favour of the government’s request for a pause and there were 306 against.
