"Rzeczpospolita": Ignoring the order of a police officer will be punished
According to the newspaper, ignoring an injunction of certain behavior issued by a policeman is to be a new type of offence, punishable by a fine, arrest or restriction of freedom.
Introducing such a change is wanted by police trade unionists.
The idea went to the Sejm’s Committee on Petitions.
“The case is serious, because already every year more than half a million offence cases go to the Polish courts,” we read.
“Rzeczpospolita” has determined that the petition, which the Independent Trade Union of the Couponers writes to the deputies regarding the amendment of the code of offences has a chance of success.
The authors of the petition propose a new wording of Article 54a of the Code of Offences.
“Anyone who does not compligate the request or command of a public official, issued within the limits and pursuant to the provisions of the Act, shall be punishable by imprisonment, a fine or a restriction of liberty.”
"Par. 3 of this provision refers to an identical punishment for those who do not follow orders will prevent or impede the performance of a service activity," the journal said.
Sociologist Andrzej Kowalski told “Rzeczpospolita” that even though he agrees with the main message of the proposal of the provision, he fears that if it has been implemented, it can be used in matters of political manifestation.
“In libertarian demonstrations, it would be unfavourable and instead of alleviating the situation to exacerbate it,” he adds.
Statistics reported by the newspaper show that 86 per cent of citizens have positively evaluated police interventions in 2018 and that 1.5 million local government interventions each month.
