Brussels Airlines Cancels 105 Flight Decommended Thomas Cook Bankruptcy
Although the British travel agency Thomas Cook has collapsed, his Belgian subsidiary Neckermann works.
But the company has a problem.
According to VRT, Brussels Airlines has announced that it will cancel its 105 flights in October due to issues related to Thomas Cook’s bankruptcy.
Neckermann also fired 75 employees.
All trips with an office that have been booked in Belgium will be canceled, but holidayers will be able to receive a refund.
The Ghent’s court also filed the bankruptcy of three Belgian daughter-in-chap companies: Thomas Cook Belgium, Thomas Cook Retail and Thomas Cook Financial Services.
Approximately 1,20,000 Belgians are abroad on trips purchased from Thomas Cook.
Another 40,000 people were supposed to leave with the office by the end of the year.
As recently as Monday spokeswoman Thomas Cook Belgium Leen Segers said the company is now “fully operational” despite the UK’s parent company’s declaration of bankruptcy, which caused anxiety among employees and customers.
The agency reassured at that time that the customers who bought the trips in it would go on vacation.
“Thom Cook, the eldest and the world’s largest travel agencies—announced bankruptcy on the night from Sunday to Monday.
The direct cause of the bankruptcy was the collapse of last resort talks with creditors and potential investors on Sunday.
Creditors have demanded that, in addition to a $900-million-pound bail-to-appoint rescue plan already agreed in August, an additional $200 million as collateral when travel agencies typically have a lower turnover.
However, the company’s problems have escalated for several years, which resulted in a debt of 1.6 billion pounds.
