Goerlitz offers free accommodation for its own review
Free accommodation in exchange for an opinion - such an unusual offer was prepared for the newcomer by the German Goerlitz.
The activities are to help in the fight against a progressive decrease in the number of inhabitants of the city.
Goerlitz is Germany's easternmost city.
The beautiful faulithic e-swealth attracts 140,000 tourists every year.
However, according to The Guardian, the city is grappling with significant problems.
The lowest wages are available in Goerlitz.
The city also struggles with a permanent decline in the number of inhabitants.
After the collapse of the Berlin Wall in 1989, most of them went to the west of the country, confining a 25% decrease in the city’s population.
In 2013 it amounted to 54,000 people.
Wanting to fight depopulation, officials came up with an idea to start offering free monthly accommodation in the city.
For so far 150 applications have been received by the programme.
Twenty-three of them were filed by residents of large cities.
People from Hungary, the Czech Republic, the United States and the United Kingdom also took an interest.
“When we applied, we thought that the program was to convince people to move to Gorlitz,” one of the project said.
“Now we feel that it doesn’t matter if we’ll stay here after four weeks or not,” he added.
The city owns 7,000 vacancies, and unemployment is three times higher than the national average.
Although the city authorities hope that some will move permanently, the main purpose of the program is to gain the opinion of the participants of the program.
Information collected from interviews and questionnaires is intended to help revitalise smaller German cities.
“We’d like to learn more about what people need, and if they decide to move – what their motivation was,” Robert Knippschild said.
The other cities have already been experimenting with ideas for luring new inhabitants, offering them incentives in the form of accommodation.
The first of them was Detroit, which introduced a program to pay promising young specialists for living and working in the city for a year.
Such solutions are used in many places, ranging from the Greek island of Antikythera to Candela in Italy or in the American Tulsa, Oklahoma.
